{"records":[{"id":"d2wzfr4twp","title":"Data for: PRDX6 dictates ferroptosis sensitivity by directing cellular selenium utilization","description":"Data for: PRDX6 dictates ferroptosis sensitivity by directing cellular selenium utilization","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-04T20:04:36.421Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d2wzfr4twp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d2wzfr4twp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Junya","last_name":"Ito","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m688f6py3x","title":"Targeting PRDX6-dependent localization and function of GPX4 enhances ferroptosis-mediated tumor suppression","description":"The relevant data of the article ：Targeting PRDX6-dependent localization and function of GPX4 enhances ferroptosis-mediated tumor suppression","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-21T15:41:48.774Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m688f6py3x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m688f6py3x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ziwen ","last_name":"li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wr9ctpc6jj","title":"SIRT1-FOXO3 Pathway reprograms fatty acid metabolism to Mitigate Ferroptosis and Fibrosis","description":"Targeting ferroptosis with reductive agents has emerged as a promising strategy to mitigate fibrotic diseases in various pathological conditions. However, while the NAD+/NADH redox pair plays a crucial role in biological processes, its connection to ferroptosis beyond FSP1 remains poorly understood. To explore underappreciated NAD+/NADH-dependent factors in the ferroptosis cascade, we conducted a compound screening of 138 NAD+ enzyme modulatory molecules and identified Sirtuin-1 (SIRT1) activators as critical regulators of ferroptosis. SIRT1 activators, such as SRT1720, broadly inhibit ferroptosis by reducing lipid peroxidation without affecting labile iron levels. Moreover, SRT1720 effectively alleviates pulmonary and liver fibrosis in mouse models. Using ChIP-seq and validation assays, we demonstrated that FOXO3 binds to gene loci involved in fatty acid elongation and arachidonic acid metabolism in ferroptosis. SRT1720 disrupts this binding, thereby restraining fatty acid metabolism and limiting lipid peroxides production. Our findings establish the SIRT1-FOXO3 axis as a key inhibitory pathway against ferroptosis and highlight its therapeutic potential for ferroptosis-associated diseases, including fibrosis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-08T17:46:01.768Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wr9ctpc6jj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wr9ctpc6jj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"songmin","last_name":"ying","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wen","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"qian","last_name":"wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"haixia","last_name":"chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Death"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f84wfs2j72","title":"GCLM lactylation mediated by ACAT2 promotes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant cancer","description":"This data consists of raw immunoblotting images from the article titled \"KGCLM lactylation mediated by ACAT2 promotes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant cancer\". KRAS mutations drive tumorigenesis, but their role in ferroptosis regulation remains unclear. Here, we construct KRASWT and KRASG12D-mutant cancer cells and demonstrate that G12D-mutant cells exhibit increased viability and reduced ferroptosis upon RSL3 or erastin treatment. These cells show diminished lipid peroxidation and mitochondrial damage, indicating ferroptosis resistance. KRASG12D activates MEK-ERK signaling to phosphorylate LDHA, enhancing glycolysis and lactate production. Exogenous lactate supplementation similarly protects wild-type cells from ferroptosis. Mechanistically, G12D mutation-derived lactate induces Glutamate-cysteine ligase modifier (GCLM) lactylation, a process catalysed by Acetyl-CoA Acetyltransferase 2 (ACAT2). Inhibition of GCLM lactylation either through the mutation of the lactylation site or by knockdown of ACAT2 diminished the enzymatic activity of Glutamate-cysteine ligase (GCL) and suppressed Glutathione synthesis. Importantly, ACAT2 depletion overcomes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant tumors in vivo. Our findings reveal a KRASG12D-driven metabolic adaptation linking GCLM lactylation to ferroptosis resistance, proposing ACAT2 inhibition as a therapeutic strategy for KRAS-mutant cancers. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-09T14:20:53.296Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f84wfs2j72","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f84wfs2j72"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"裕斌","last_name":"陈","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"58chtf922v","title":"GCLM lactylation mediated by ACAT2 promotes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant cancer","description":"This data consists of raw immunoblotting images from the article titled \"KGCLM lactylation mediated by ACAT2 promotes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant cancer\". KRAS mutations drive tumorigenesis, but their role in ferroptosis regulation remains unclear. Here, we construct KRASWT and KRASG12D-mutant cancer cells and demonstrate that G12D-mutant cells exhibit increased viability and reduced ferroptosis upon RSL3 or erastin treatment. These cells show diminished lipid peroxidation and mitochondrial damage, indicating ferroptosis resistance. KRASG12D activates MEK-ERK signaling to phosphorylate LDHA, enhancing glycolysis and lactate production. Exogenous lactate supplementation similarly protects wild-type cells from ferroptosis. Mechanistically, G12D mutation-derived lactate induces Glutamate-cysteine ligase modifier (GCLM) lactylation, a process catalysed by Acetyl-CoA Acetyltransferase 2 (ACAT2). Inhibition of GCLM lactylation either through the mutation of the lactylation site or by knockdown of ACAT2 diminished the enzymatic activity of Glutamate-cysteine ligase (GCL) and suppressed Glutathione synthesis. Importantly, ACAT2 depletion overcomes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant tumors in vivo. Our findings reveal a KRASG12D-driven metabolic adaptation linking GCLM lactylation to ferroptosis resistance, proposing ACAT2 inhibition as a therapeutic strategy for KRAS-mutant cancers. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-06T05:22:29.855Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/58chtf922v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/58chtf922v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"裕斌","last_name":"陈","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ngttmkkhkn","title":"ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer","description":"Ferroptosis presents great potential for cancer therapy, either alone or in combination with classical therapy. However, inducing ferroptosis by targeting canonical ferroptosis suppressors that directly inhibit lipid peroxidation non-selectively induces ferroptosis in both cancerous and normal cells, thereby limiting its therapeutic potential. In this study, we reveal that aldolase A (ALDOA) reprograms lipid metabolism to resist ferroptosis in cancer cells and identify ALDOA as a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization. Cancer cells with ALDOA suppression exhibit increased susceptibility to ferroptosis-a response less obvious in normal cells. Mechanistically, ALDOA depletion induces significant accumulation of fructose 1,6-bisphosphate in cancer cells, thereby enhancing autophagy-dependent degradation of phospholipid-modifying enzymes. These alterations increase the ratio of phospholipids containing pro-ferroptotic polyunsaturated fatty acids over anti-ferroptotic monounsaturated fatty acids, culminating in heightened ferroptosis sensitivity. Moreover, ALDOA inhibitors selectively promote ferroptosis in cancer cells, both in vitro and in vivo. Collectively, our findings reveal that ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-27T01:37:28.511Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ngttmkkhkn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ngttmkkhkn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"pengqi","last_name":"wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"sheng","last_name":"ding","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Tsinghua-Peking Center for Life Sciences","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"Beijing Natural Science Foundation","grant_id":"JQ22016"},{"id":null,"name":"New Cornerstone Investigator Program","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"National Key R&D Program of China ","grant_id":"2022YFA1103704"}],"categories":["Lipidomics","Metabolomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cp8cvzx22z","title":"OPA1 promotes ferroptosis by augmenting mitochondrial ROS and suppressing an integrated stress response","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent form of nonapoptotic cell death mediated by lipid peroxidation, has been implicated in the pathogenesis of multiple diseases. Subcellular organelles play pivotal roles in the regulation of ferroptosis, but the mechanisms underlying the contributions of the mitochondria remain poorly defined. Optic atrophy 1 (OPA1) is a mitochondrial dynamin-like GTPase that controls mitochondrial morphogenesis, fusion, and energetics. Here, we report that human and mouse cells lacking OPA1 are markedly resistant to ferroptosis. Reconstitution with OPA1 mutants demonstrates that ferroptosis sensitization requires the GTPase activity but is independent of OPA1-mediated mitochondrial fusion. Mechanistically, OPA1 confers susceptibility to ferroptosis by maintaining mitochondrial homeostasis and function, which contributes both to the generation of mitochondrial lipid reactive oxygen species (ROS) and suppression of an ATF4-mediated integrated stress response. Together, these results identify an OPA1-controlled mitochondrial axis of ferroptosis regulation and provide mechanistic insights for therapeutically manipulating this form of cell death in diseases.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-13T17:28:29.217Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cp8cvzx22z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cp8cvzx22z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Felix ","last_name":"Liang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mmnwww6n3s","title":"LZTFL1 Rewires NADPH–Glutathione Metabolism to Amplify Ferroptosis","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent form of regulated cell death, represents a therapeutic vulnerability in apoptosis-resistant cancers. Here, we identify leucine zipper transcription factor-like 1 (LZTFL1) as a key regulator of ferroptosis that rewires glutathione (GSH) metabolism. A genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen and multi-omics analysis revealed that LZTFL1 promotes oxidation of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD), limiting NADPH production and GSH regeneration. GSH depletion in turn enhanced LZTFL1 translation via an AKT–mTOR–eIF4E pathway, establishing a feed-forward loop that amplifies ferroptosis. In vivo, the LZTFL1–FHOD1–G6PD axis sensitized xenograft and patient-derived xenograft models to ferroptosis, correlating with lipid peroxidation, reduced GSH, and extended survival. Clinically, LZTFL1 expression restored cisplatin efficacy in resistant lung and ovarian cancer cells and predicted better survival outcomes in LUAD patients. Moreover, FDA-approved compounds upregulated LZTFL1 and re-sensitized resistant tumors to cisplatin. These findings define LZTFL1 as both a biomarker and therapeutic target for enhancing ferroptosis-based cancer therapy. The files below contain raw western blot images and flow cytometry data from this study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-12T06:59:22.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mmnwww6n3s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mmnwww6n3s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"XIANGFEI","last_name":"XUE","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Flowmetry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"95wwrptr59","title":"Lipid availability influences ferroptosis sensitivity in cancer cells by regulating polyunsaturated fatty acid trafficking","description":"Ferroptosis is a form of cell death caused by lipid peroxidation that is emerging as a target for cancer therapy, highlighting the need to identify factors that govern ferroptosis susceptibility. Lipid peroxidation occurs primarily on phospholipids containing polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs). Here, we show that even though extracellular lipid limitation reduces cellular PUFA levels, lipid-starved cancer cells are paradoxically more sensitive to ferroptosis. Using mass spectrometry-based lipidomics with stable isotope fatty acid labeling, we show that lipid limitation induces a fatty acid trafficking pathway in which PUFAs are liberated from triglycerides to synthesize highly unsaturated PUFAs such as arachidonic and adrenic acid. These PUFAs then accumulate in phospholipids, including ether phospholipids, to promote ferroptosis sensitivity. Therefore, PUFA levels within cancer cells do not necessarily correlate with ferroptosis susceptibility. Rather, how cancer cells respond to extracellular lipid levels by trafficking PUFAs into proper phospholipid pools contributes to their sensitivity to ferroptosis.  \n\nThis data set includes uncropped Western blot images from this manuscript.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-16T17:49:29.291Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/95wwrptr59","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/95wwrptr59"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Evan","last_name":"Lien","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R00CA255928"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"5T32CA251066-03"}],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gbvp7h5h8j","title":"Nucleotide Biosynthesis Links Glutathione Metabolism to Ferroptosis Sensitivity","description":"Previous tab is gene-level FPKM counts derived from RNA sequencing in unmodified HT-1080 control (Control) cells, HT-1080 TP53 gene-disrupted (p53^KO) cells, and HT-1080 CDKN1A gene disrupted (p21^KO) cell lines treated with or without nutlin-3 (10 µM, 48 h) prior to sample harvest. Two independent biological replicates per condition (Rep #1, Rep #2) are reported.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-12-03T11:06:27.739Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gbvp7h5h8j","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gbvp7h5h8j"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Dixon","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":126281,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":135,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":126281,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":125693,"restricted":66,"embargoed":522},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2166},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":658},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":446},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":408},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":406},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":396},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":386},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":312},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":252},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":216}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":123767},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1807},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":181},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":130},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":73},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":52},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":26}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":480},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":405},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":357},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":326},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":291}]}}